envelope: 1
uuid: nexus-export-schema-dom
type: schema
id: dom
context: >-
  lib/node-schemas — shared canonical registry. Grounded in clear-glass/src/dom/archaeology.js's
  real handlePick() record, confirmed by direct read of that method this pass.
intent: null
summary: null
system: clear-glass
tags: []
exported_at: 0
source: nexus.lib.node-export
payload:
  status: REAL
  source: >-
    clear-glass/src/dom/archaeology.js's real handlePick() row — {pickId, agentId, nodeId, name,
    meta, pickedAt} — plus the `selector`/`url` that _getWebContents and the dom:pick-result IPC
    payload already carry. §MCO17 2026-09-14 — NODE-TAXONOMY.md previously recorded `.dom` as
    "checked, neither built... DOM queries are live and ephemeral, never persisted." The first
    half of that was right about DOM QUERIES (handleQuery's tree/selector reads genuinely are
    transient results, and are still not node-worthy). The second half reasoned from an
    implementation detail to an architectural conclusion: a PICK is not ephemeral by nature, it
    is ephemeral because `this.picks` is an in-memory Map that dies with the process. That file's
    own header calls picks "registered as named hooks" — a named, addressable handle onto a live
    page that cannot survive a restart is a §2.1/§2.2 violation, not a design decision. This is
    that handle given a durable form. Deliberately NOT the same type as `.response`: a `.response`
    is one moment in a guardian job's answer arriving; a `.dom` is a durable clear-glass-owned
    address. Two subjects, two owners, two write authorities (§10.1).
  fields:
    pickId:
      type: string
      required: true
      description: real unique id for this pick — archaeology.js's own uuidv4(), reused verbatim
    name:
      type: string
      required: true
      description: >-
        the real human/agent-facing handle, e.g. 'submit-button'. archaeology.js defaults this to
        `pick-N` when the caller supplies nothing; that default is preserved rather than made
        required, because a caller that didn't name a pick genuinely didn't, and inventing a
        better name here would misrepresent what happened
    agentId:
      type: string
      required: false
      description: which real provider tab this element was picked in
    nodeId:
      type: string
      required: false
      description: the real __cgDomMesh node id inside the injected observer
    selector:
      type: string
      required: false
      description: >-
        real CSS selector for the element when one was resolved. Optional and honest about why:
        the DOM mesh addresses by cgId first, and a selector is not always derivable
    url:
      type: string
      required: false
      description: real page URL the pick was taken on — a pick is only meaningful against a page
    meta:
      type: object
      required: false
      description: real, pick-specific data carried through from the renderer unchanged
    pickedAt:
      type: number
      required: true
      description: real pick timestamp — logical ordering axis (§3.2)
    lastSeenAt:
      type: number
      required: false
      description: >-
        real timestamp the selector was last confirmed to still resolve on the live page. Absent
        means never re-checked, which is different from "checked and still valid" and is kept
        distinguishable rather than defaulted to pickedAt
